    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <ul class="faq-list">
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Does salary sacrifice reduce my taxable income?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Yes. When you salary sacrifice to super, the sacrificed amount is not included in your assessable income for the year. Your employer pays income tax on the reduced gross salary, which means less PAYG withholding from each payslip. At the end of the year, your payment summary reflects the lower taxable amount, and you pay less income tax overall.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Is there a limit on how much I can salary sacrifice to super?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">The limit is the concessional contributions cap, which is $30,000 per financial year for FY2025-26. This cap covers all pre-tax super contributions — your employer's SG plus anything you salary sacrifice. On a $100,000 salary with 12% SG, your maximum salary sacrifice is $18,000. Going above the cap means the excess is taxed at your marginal rate plus an interest charge.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Can casual or part-time workers salary sacrifice to super?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Yes, salary sacrifice is available to any employee whose employer offers it, regardless of whether you work full-time, part-time, or on a casual basis. What changes is the dollar amount available — because your SG contributions will be lower, you may have more cap space to sacrifice. The same rules around the $30,000 concessional cap apply.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">What happens to my salary sacrifice contributions if I change jobs?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Your super balance, including all salary sacrifice contributions, stays in your nominated super fund regardless of where you work. When you move to a new employer, simply provide them with your fund details and set up a new salary sacrifice arrangement. Be careful to account for the SG contributions from both employers when calculating your remaining concessional cap space for that financial year.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Does salary sacrifice affect my employer's super guarantee obligations?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">No — since 2020, employers are legally required to calculate SG contributions on your original ordinary time earnings, not your reduced post-sacrifice salary. This prevents salary sacrifice from being used to reduce the employer's SG obligation. If your employer is calculating SG on the sacrificed salary, that is a compliance breach you should raise with them or report to the ATO.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Is salary sacrifice the same as a personal super contribution?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">No. A salary sacrifice is made before income tax is applied, so it is treated as an employer contribution (taxed at 15% in the fund). A personal super contribution is made from your after-tax take-home pay. You may be able to claim a tax deduction on personal contributions by lodging a Notice of Intent to Claim form with your fund — but the process is different, and they are not the same as salary sacrifice.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

